{"meta": {"course": "ai-ethics", "chapter": "04", "section": "01", "chapter_title": "Privacy, Consent, and Data Governance", "title": "Module 1: Privacy Threats from AI Systems", "description": "Identify four distinct privacy threats (re-identification, inference attacks, training-data leakage, surveillance-by-aggregation) and match each to its appropriate countermeasure (k-anonymity, differential privacy, federated learning, data minimization).", "learning_objectives": ["Distinguish re-identification, membership inference, attribute inference, and model inversion attacks.", "Recognize when removing direct identifiers is insufficient for anonymization.", "Match each privacy threat to an appropriate technical or governance countermeasure.", "Apply GDPR principles (data minimization, purpose limitation, retention limits) to AI deployment design."], "concept_ids": ["re_identification", "membership_inference", "training_data_leakage", "differential_privacy", "federated_learning", "data_minimization"], "prereq_section_ids": ["ai-ethics/03/02"], "practice_ids": ["privacy_anonymization_limit", "privacy_membership_inference", "privacy_dp_purpose", "privacy_aggregation_threat"], "estimated_time_minutes": 13, "order": 1}, "content": "# Privacy in the Age of AI: Why \"We Anonymized the Data\" Is Almost Never the End of the Story\n\n> **\"Anonymized data isn't.\"**\n> - Cynthia Dwork, co-inventor of differential privacy, paraphrasing a hard-won lesson from a decade of re-identification results.\n\nIn October 2006, AOL released what it believed was a harmless research gift: roughly 20 million search queries from 650,000 users, with usernames replaced by random numbers. Within days, *New York Times* reporters had used the searches themselves (\"landscapers in Lilburn, GA,\" \"homes sold in shadow lake subdivision,\" \"60 single men\") to knock on the door of user No. 4417749, a 62-year-old widow named Thelma Arnold. No name was ever in the file. The behavior was the identifier. AOL pulled the dataset, its CTO resigned, and the episode became the textbook proof that stripping identifiers is not the same as protecting people.\n\nThat lesson has only sharpened under AI. The AOL release was queried by humans with manual tools. Modern AI systems are trained on large, joinable, behavior-rich data and queried at scale by models that learn statistical regularities humans cannot see, and that can be coaxed to repeat what they memorized. Treating privacy as a single problem (\"anonymization\") with a single fix is the most common privacy failure in AI deployments.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Distinguish** the four structurally different privacy threats AI systems create (re-identification, model inference, generative leakage, surveillance-by-aggregation) and name the countermeasure family that targets each.\n2. **Explain** why removing direct identifiers fails, using the quasi-identifier and auxiliary-data linkage mechanism.\n3. **Interpret** the differential-privacy parameter $\\varepsilon$ as a leakage budget and reason about the privacy/utility tradeoff it encodes.\n4. **Apply** the threat taxonomy to a concrete deployment using a stakeholder and tradeoff decision table.\n5. **Argue** why legal compliance (GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA) is necessary but not sufficient for ethical privacy.\n\n---\n\n## The Core Idea: Privacy Is Plural\n\nThere is no single \"privacy\" property to switch on. The privacy landscape under AI has at least four distinct threats, each with its own technical countermeasure and its own legal framing. The rest of this section walks each one, then assembles them into a worked deployment analysis.\n\nThis module covers re-identification, inference attacks, training-data leakage, and surveillance-by-aggregation, plus the basic toolbox of countermeasures (differential privacy, federated learning, encrypted computation, synthetic data), grounded in the GDPR, the FTC privacy framework, and the NIST Privacy Framework.\n\n---\n\n## Threat 1: Re-Identification\n\nRemoving direct identifiers (name, SSN) does not anonymize a dataset that contains many other features. Latanya Sweeney showed in the late 1990s that 87% of US residents could be uniquely identified by zip code, birth date, and gender alone. Modern datasets contain dozens of joinable features.\n\n**Mechanism.** An attacker has auxiliary data (a voter roll, a movie-rental dataset, a hospital release) and links it to the supposedly anonymous dataset using shared features, called *quasi-identifiers*. Once linked, the previously de-identified records become identified.\n\n**Famous cases.** The AOL search-log release (2006, above). The Netflix Prize re-identification (Narayanan and Shmatikov, 2008), which linked anonymized movie ratings to public IMDb reviews. The MA Group Insurance Commission release that Sweeney used to re-identify the medical records of the Massachusetts governor.\n\n**Countermeasure.** *k*-anonymity (each record indistinguishable from at least $k-1$ others on quasi-identifiers) and its successors *l*-diversity and *t*-closeness. These reduce but do not eliminate re-identification risk; for high-dimensional behavioral data they often fail, because almost every long behavioral record is unique.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A dataset has direct identifiers removed but retains 5-digit zip, full birth date, and gender. Why does this still permit re-identification?\" explanation=\"Right. Sweeney showed zip + birth date + gender uniquely identifies about 87% of US residents. These are quasi-identifiers: not identifying alone, but jointly linkable to an auxiliary dataset (voter rolls, obituaries) to pin down a record.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Those three fields are themselves direct identifiers like names.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"They are quasi-identifiers whose combination is near-unique and can be linked to an auxiliary dataset to re-identify records.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Zip codes are public, so the whole record becomes public.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## Threat 2: Inference Attacks Against the Model\n\nEven if the training data is never released, the **trained model itself** can leak information about training subjects.\n\n**Membership inference attacks** (Shokri et al., 2017): given access to a trained model, an attacker can determine whether a particular individual's record was in the training set. For some medical or rare-disease datasets, just being known to be in the training set is itself a privacy harm (it discloses the diagnosis).\n\n**Attribute inference attacks**: given a model and partial information about a person, infer attributes the person did not disclose. A recommender model can leak political affiliation; a health-risk model can leak diagnosis.\n\n**Model inversion attacks** (Fredrikson et al., 2015): reconstruct training inputs from model parameters. Demonstrated for face recognition and for genomic data.\n\n**Countermeasure.** *Differential privacy* (Dwork et al., 2006) adds calibrated noise to training so that the trained model's behavior changes only marginally if any one individual is removed from the dataset. The privacy parameter $\\varepsilon$ quantifies the leakage; smaller $\\varepsilon$ is stronger privacy at the cost of model utility.\n\n> **Reading $\\varepsilon$.** The formal guarantee bounds how much any single record can shift the model's output distribution:\n> $$ \\Pr[M(D) \\in S] \\le e^{\\varepsilon}\\,\\Pr[M(D') \\in S] $$\n> for any two datasets $D, D'$ differing in one person's record and any outcome set $S$. At $\\varepsilon = 0$ the two distributions are identical (perfect privacy, useless model); as $\\varepsilon \\to \\infty$ there is no constraint. The factor $e^{\\varepsilon}$ is the worst-case multiplicative \"tell.\" For intuition: $\\varepsilon = 1$ gives $e^1 \\approx 2.72$, so any one person can shift an outcome's probability by at most a factor of about 2.7; $\\varepsilon = 0.1$ gives $e^{0.1} \\approx 1.11$, a much tighter bound (\u224811% shift) but noisier, less accurate training. Apple has historically run telemetry at single-digit $\\varepsilon$ per datum; the 2020 US Census used a budget allocated across the whole publication. $\\varepsilon$ is a spendable budget, not a setting you get for free.\n\nManipulate the leakage budget directly. The worst-case multiplicative \"tell\" is $e^{\\varepsilon}$; a rough utility proxy is how much calibrated noise you must inject, which scales with the query sensitivity divided by the budget. Watch how a tighter budget shrinks the privacy tell but blows up the noise:\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Differential Privacy: budget vs. leakage vs. noise\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"epsilon\",\"label\":\"Privacy budget (epsilon)\",\"min\":0.05,\"max\":5,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":1},{\"name\":\"sensitivity\",\"label\":\"Query sensitivity (delta-f)\",\"min\":1,\"max\":20,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":5}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Worst-case multiplicative tell e^epsilon\",\"unit\":\"x\",\"expr\":\"2.718281828^epsilon\"},{\"label\":\"Laplace noise scale b = sensitivity/epsilon\",\"unit\":\"\",\"expr\":\"sensitivity/epsilon\"},{\"label\":\"Max probability shift\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"(2.718281828^epsilon - 1)*100\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"epsilon\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nNotice the asymmetry: pushing $\\varepsilon$ from 1 down to 0.1 only tightens the tell modestly, but the noise scale grows tenfold. That nonlinearity is exactly why small clinical cohorts get wrecked by strict budgets.\n\n---\n\n## Threat 3: Training-Data Leakage in Generative Models\n\nLarge generative models present a distinct threat: they can be **prompted to reproduce training data verbatim**. Carlini et al. (2021, 2023) showed that GPT-2 and Stable Diffusion both leak training data, including PII, when probed adversarially.\n\n**Mechanism.** Memorization in over-parameterized models is not a bug; it is part of how they fit rare patterns. The training distribution includes specific phone numbers, copyrighted text, faces, and medical records that the model can be coaxed to emit.\n\n**Countermeasure.** Training-data curation, differential privacy at training (DP-SGD), and output filtering at inference. None is a complete fix; the field is still working on this. The legal scaffolding (CCPA right to deletion, GDPR right to erasure) creates obligations that current generative model architectures cannot cleanly satisfy: you cannot easily \"delete\" one person from a 70-billion-parameter model without retraining.\n\n---\n\n## Threat 4: Surveillance-by-Aggregation\n\nA single data point is not invasive. A million data points across years is. AI systems are uniquely positioned to combine streams (location, communication metadata, biometrics, behavioral patterns) that individually are mundane and collectively reveal far more than any individual stream.\n\nThis is the threat behind Cambridge Analytica, behind FTC enforcement actions on advertising data brokers, and behind the EU's Digital Services Act surveillance-advertising restrictions. The harm is not the breach of any single record; it is the **profile that can be assembled** when many records are joined.\n\n**Countermeasure.** Data minimization (collect only what the use case requires), purpose limitation (use data only for the disclosed purpose), and retention limits (delete when the purpose is served). These are GDPR principles, not just AI-specific norms; AI deployments inherit them.\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"privacy-threat-taxonomy\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## Tools: A Quick Map\n\n**Differential privacy** is the strongest formal privacy guarantee available. It bounds the leakage from a single individual's record. Used in production by Apple (telemetry), the US Census Bureau (2020 decennial), and increasingly in ML training (DP-SGD).\n\n**Federated learning** keeps raw data on user devices and aggregates gradients. Reduces server-side data exposure but does not by itself eliminate inference attacks; usually combined with DP.\n\n**Encrypted computation** (homomorphic encryption, secure multi-party computation) lets parties compute on data without decrypting it. Mature for specific use cases (privacy-preserving analytics, federated training).\n\n**Synthetic data** generates statistically similar data that does not refer to real individuals. Useful for testing and some training, but synthetic data can still leak when the generator overfits.\n\nNone of these tools is a drop-in fix. Each has utility costs and threat-model assumptions. Choosing among them is part of the privacy-engineering job, not a checkbox.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Case Analysis: The \"De-Identified\" Hospital Dataset\n\n**Scenario.** A health-system vendor proposes to share a \"de-identified\" patient dataset with an outside research lab to train a readmission-risk model. The vendor has removed names, SSNs, and medical-record numbers. The dataset still contains, per patient: diagnosis codes, admission and discharge dates, 5-digit zip code, age, and gender. The lab will publish the trained model as an API.\n\nBefore reading the analysis, predict the threat profile yourself. Each step asks you to commit to an answer, then reveals the reasoning:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Diagnose the de-identified hospital dataset\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The vendor kept diagnosis codes, admission/discharge dates, 5-digit zip, age, and gender. Which single threat is MOST acutely exposed?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Generative verbatim leakage\",\"Re-identification via quasi-identifiers\",\"No threat: identifiers were removed\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Re-identification. Zip + age + gender is Sweeney 87%-unique combination, and admission dates add further uniqueness, all joinable to voter rolls or obituaries.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The lab will publish the trained model as a public API. What NEW threat does publishing the model (not the data) create?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Membership inference\",\"Surveillance-by-aggregation\",\"None: the data stays private\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Membership inference. A queryable model API lets an attacker test whether a known person was in the training set, which itself discloses they had the diagnosis, even though the raw table was never shared.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Generative/verbatim leakage is LOW here: a tabular risk classifier is not generative. Surveillance-by-aggregation is MODERATE and depends on whether the lab joins this feed with others; purpose limitation is the relevant control. The lesson: run the scenario against all four threats, not the single question is it anonymized.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n**Step 1: Name the threats.** Run the scenario against all four threats rather than asking the single question \"is it anonymized?\"\n\n| Threat | Exposed here? | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Re-identification | **Yes, high** | Zip + date-of-birth-equivalent (age) + gender is exactly Sweeney's 87%-unique combination; admission dates add even more uniqueness. Joinable to voter rolls or news obituaries. |\n| Membership inference | **Yes** | A published model API lets an attacker test whether a known person was in the training set, which itself discloses they had the diagnosis. |\n| Generative/verbatim leakage | **Low** | A tabular risk classifier is not generative; verbatim emission is unlikely (would rise if the lab used a generative model). |\n| Surveillance-by-aggregation | **Moderate** | If the lab joins this with other feeds, the per-patient profile deepens; purpose limitation is the relevant control. |\n\n**Step 2: Map stakeholders and what each stands to lose or gain.**\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest | Primary risk |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Patients | Care quality, confidentiality | Re-identification of a sensitive diagnosis; loss of insurability or employment |\n| Research lab | Model accuracy, publishability | Liability if re-identification occurs; weaker model if data is degraded |\n| Hospital/vendor | Reputation, HIPAA compliance, revenue | Regulatory penalty; breach-of-trust headline |\n| Regulator (OCR/FTC) | Enforce HIPAA/Section 5 | Public harm if enforcement fails |\n\n**Step 3: Decide, with the tradeoff made explicit.** The naive plan (\"share the de-identified table\") fails on Threats 1 and 2. A defensible alternative ranks countermeasures by where they cut risk:\n\n| Option | Privacy gain | Utility cost | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Ship table as-is | None on Threats 1-2 | None | Reject |\n| Generalize quasi-identifiers (zip \u2192 3-digit, age \u2192 10-yr band) + *k*-anonymity, $k=10$ | Cuts re-identification | Some signal loss | Necessary, not sufficient (model still leaks via Threat 2) |\n| Train with DP-SGD at a stated $\\varepsilon$ budget; publish only the API | Bounds membership/attribute inference | Accuracy drop, larger budget needed | Add this |\n| Keep raw data on hospital servers; lab trains via federated learning + DP | Removes raw-data transfer (Threat 4) | Engineering cost, slower | Best if feasible |\n\n**Decision.** Do not transfer the raw table. Generalize quasi-identifiers to clear *k*-anonymity for the worst case, train under differential privacy with a documented $\\varepsilon$, and prefer a federated arrangement so the raw records never leave the covered entity. Document a HIPAA business-associate agreement and a purpose-limitation clause forbidding re-joining with other datasets. The point of the table is that no single tool suffices; the four threats require a *layered* response.\n\nNow you make the calls. You are the privacy engineer on this project and the deadline is tight. Trace your choices to their consequences:\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"You are the privacy engineer for the hospital data share\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The research lab asks for the de-identified table today so it can hit a grant deadline. What do you do first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Ship the table as-is; identifiers were removed\",\"next\":\"ship\",\"feedback\":\"You treated de-identification as anonymization.\"},{\"label\":\"Refuse to transfer raw records and propose a layered plan\",\"next\":\"layer\",\"feedback\":\"Good: you recognized the four threats need a layered response.\"},{\"label\":\"Generalize zip and age, then ship the table\",\"next\":\"kanon\",\"feedback\":\"k-anonymity helps Threat 1 but ignores the model leak.\"}]},\"ship\":{\"prompt\":\"Six months later a journalist links a published readmission record to an obituary and names a patient. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Invoke algorithmic disgorgement and delete the model\",\"next\":\"disgorge\",\"feedback\":\"The FTC remedy you should have avoided needing.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Re-identification via quasi-identifiers (Threat 1) was foreseeable and high; shipping unprotected breached HIPAA and trust.\"},\"kanon\":{\"prompt\":\"The published model API is now live. An attacker runs membership-inference queries. What did generalization miss?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Add DP-SGD training and re-release the API\",\"next\":\"layer\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: the model itself leaks (Threat 2); k-anonymity on the table cannot bound that.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Generalizing quasi-identifiers cut Threat 1 but left Threat 2 (membership inference) wide open because the leak is in the trained model, not the table.\"},\"layer\":{\"prompt\":\"You propose: generalize quasi-identifiers to k=10, train with DP-SGD at a documented epsilon, keep raw data on hospital servers via federated learning, and sign a purpose-limitation BAA. The lab says DP noise hurts accuracy. Your call?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Negotiate epsilon openly, report it, and accept some cohorts are too small to do privately\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"This is the honest tradeoff: transparency over a hidden compromise.\"},{\"label\":\"Quietly raise epsilon to whatever the lab wants for accuracy\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"Tempting, but an undocumented budget defeats the point; the principled move is to report it.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"Outcome: raw records never left the covered entity, membership inference is bounded, and the epsilon budget is documented and auditable. No single tool did this; the layers did.\",\"feedback\":\"You delivered a defensible, layered deployment.\",\"choices\":[]},\"disgorge\":{\"prompt\":\"The model is deleted, the lab loses its work, and the hospital faces an OCR inquiry. Every layered control you skipped would have been cheaper than this.\",\"feedback\":\"Layered prevention beats post-hoc disgorgement.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Counterarguments and Limitations\n\n- **\"Differential privacy is too lossy for medicine.\"** Sometimes true. Small clinical cohorts can be wrecked by the noise a strict $\\varepsilon$ requires. The honest response is not to abandon DP but to negotiate $\\varepsilon$ openly, report it, and accept that some studies are too small to do privately at all.\n- **\"k-anonymity is enough.\"** It is not, against an attacker with strong auxiliary data or against the trained model's own leakage; *l*-diversity and *t*-closeness patch some gaps but high-dimensional behavioral data defeats all of them.\n- **\"Synthetic data sidesteps the whole problem.\"** Only if the generator does not overfit. A generator that memorizes can reproduce real records; synthetic data needs its own privacy audit.\n- **\"We are GDPR-compliant, so we are fine.\"** Compliance is a floor, not a ceiling. A GDPR-compliant deployment that produces surveillance-grade profiles can still be ethically wrong, which the next subsection makes precise.\n\n---\n\n## Legal Floor: GDPR, CCPA, FTC\n\n**GDPR** (EU, 2018) defines data minimization, purpose limitation, lawful basis, and rights to access, rectification, and erasure. It treats inferred data the same as collected data, which has direct AI implications.\n\n**CCPA / CPRA** (California, 2020 / 2023) gives consumers rights to know, delete, and opt out of sale of personal information. Increasingly relevant for AI training data.\n\n**FTC** has used Section 5 (unfair and deceptive practices) to enforce against companies whose AI deployments produced privacy harms (Rite Aid facial recognition, Everalbum face data), including the remedy of *algorithmic disgorgement*: ordering the deletion of models trained on improperly collected data.\n\nThese do not exhaust the ethics. A legally compliant deployment can still be ethically wrong. Compliance is necessary; not sufficient.\n\n---\n\n## Concept Map\n\nThe six load-bearing ideas of this section: three of the threat families and the three countermeasure families they call for. Trace which countermeasure answers which threat.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"re_identification\",\"membership_inference\",\"training_data_leakage\",\"differential_privacy\",\"federated_learning\",\"data_minimization\"]'></concept-map>\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n- **Privacy is plural.** AI creates four structurally different threats (re-identification, model inference, generative leakage, surveillance-by-aggregation); each needs its own countermeasure family.\n- **Identifiers are not the boundary of identity.** Behavior re-identifies people (AOL, Netflix, Sweeney); quasi-identifiers plus auxiliary data defeat naive de-identification.\n- **The model leaks, not just the data.** Membership, attribute, and inversion attacks operate even when training data is never released.\n- **$\\varepsilon$ is a budget.** Differential privacy makes the privacy/utility tradeoff explicit and quantifiable; smaller $\\varepsilon$ means stronger privacy and a noisier, less accurate model.\n- **Layer the defenses.** Real deployments combine generalization, DP, federation, and contractual purpose limits; no single tool is a fix.\n- **Compliance is the floor, not the goal.** GDPR/CCPA/HIPAA are necessary but not sufficient for ethical privacy.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nThe next section moves from *protecting* individuals in data to *fairness* in the decisions a model makes about them: how bias enters a pipeline, how to measure it, and why several reasonable fairness definitions provably cannot all hold at once. Privacy and fairness frequently trade off (the noise that protects privacy can degrade accuracy unevenly across groups), so the tools introduced here reappear there as constraints.\n\n---\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"AI Privacy\" context=\"A health-system vendor proposes to share 'de-identified' patient data with a research lab for AI training. The dataset includes diagnoses, dates, and zip codes. Walk through which of the four privacy threats this proposal exposes patients to, what countermeasures would mitigate each, and what GDPR or HIPAA obligations apply.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Why is removing names and SSNs typically insufficient for anonymizing a behavioral dataset used in AI training?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because names and SSNs are not personal data.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because remaining quasi-identifiers (zip code, birth date, gender, behavioral signatures) can be linked to auxiliary datasets to re-identify records, and the trained model itself can leak training subjects via membership inference, attribute inference, or memorized verbatim outputs.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because SSNs are public.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. Re-identification, membership inference, and training-data leakage all operate after direct identifiers are removed. Anonymization is a high bar that simple field removal does not clear.\"></interactive-quiz>\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "privacy_anonymization_limit", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "re_identification", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["re_identification"], "stem": "Why is removing names and SSNs typically insufficient to anonymize a behavioral dataset?", "options": ["Because names and SSNs are not personal data.", "Because remaining quasi-identifiers (zip, birth date, gender, behavioral patterns) can link to auxiliary data and re-identify records. Sweeney showed 87% of US residents are uniquely identified by zip+DOB+gender.", "Because the dataset is too small.", "Because deep learning ignores identifiers."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Quasi-identifiers compose into unique fingerprints. Re-identification is well-documented; field removal alone does not anonymize.", "misconception_id": "field_removal_anonymizes"}, {"id": "privacy_membership_inference", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "membership_inference", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["membership_inference", "training_data_leakage"], "stem": "What is a membership inference attack?", "options": ["Stealing a model's weights.", "Determining whether a particular individual's record was in the training set, given access to the trained model. For some datasets, just being in the training set is itself a privacy harm.", "Inferring a person's name from their face.", "Reverse-engineering the loss function."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Shokri et al. (2017) demonstrated that trained models leak membership information. For sensitive datasets (rare diseases, abuse-victim registries), training-set inclusion is itself harmful.", "misconception_id": "models_dont_leak"}, {"id": "privacy_dp_purpose", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "differential_privacy", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["differential_privacy"], "stem": "What does differential privacy formally guarantee?", "options": ["Perfect anonymity with no utility loss.", "That the output of an analysis or trained model changes only marginally if any one individual's record is added or removed, bounded by a privacy parameter \u03b5. Smaller \u03b5 = stronger privacy at higher utility cost.", "That data is encrypted.", "That data is deleted after use."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Differential privacy bounds per-individual leakage. It is the strongest formal privacy guarantee available, with a quantified utility-privacy trade-off.", "misconception_id": "dp_is_perfect"}, {"id": "privacy_aggregation_threat", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "data_minimization", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["data_minimization"], "stem": "What is the surveillance-by-aggregation threat, and which GDPR principles most directly counter it?", "options": ["A type of brute-force attack; countered by encryption.", "Combining multiple individually mundane data streams (location, communication, biometrics, behavior) yields profiles that reveal far more than any single stream. Countered by data minimization, purpose limitation, and retention limits.", "An attack only on advertising systems.", "Not a real threat."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Aggregation transforms benign-seeming data into invasive profiles. GDPR's data minimization, purpose limitation, and retention limits are designed to bound exactly this risk.", "misconception_id": "single_record_view"}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "field_removal_anonymizes", "pattern": "weak_anonymization", "description": "Believing removing names and SSNs is sufficient to anonymize a behavioral dataset", "trigger": "learner says 'we removed PII, the dataset is anonymous'", "feedback": "Quasi-identifiers (zip, DOB, gender, behavioral patterns) can link to auxiliary data and re-identify records. Sweeney's 87% result is the canonical demonstration.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "models_dont_leak", "pattern": "model_as_safe", "description": "Assuming trained models cannot leak training data after the data is locked away", "trigger": "learner releases a model trained on sensitive data without privacy auditing", "feedback": "Models leak via membership inference, attribute inference, and verbatim memorization. Releasing a model is closer to releasing a lossy view of the training data than to releasing nothing.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "dp_is_perfect", "pattern": "guarantee_overstatement", "description": "Believing differential privacy provides perfect anonymity with no utility cost", "trigger": "learner cites DP as a one-size-fits-all privacy fix", "feedback": "DP bounds per-individual leakage by a parameter \u03b5. Smaller \u03b5 means stronger privacy and lower utility. Choosing \u03b5 is itself a policy decision, not a technical default.", "rail_action": "practice"}, {"id": "single_record_view", "pattern": "aggregation_blindness", "description": "Evaluating privacy harm one record at a time without considering aggregation", "trigger": "learner argues 'each data point is mundane, so the system is not invasive'", "feedback": "Joining mundane streams produces invasive profiles. Surveillance-by-aggregation is the threat that data minimization, purpose limitation, and retention limits are designed to bound.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}